   "text": {
    "L1": "A rectangle has two diagonals. They are equal in length, and they cut each other exactly in half.",
    "L2": "But here is a myth: every rectangle's diagonals meet at right angles. That is not true; only a square has that property.",
    "L3": "Now take two points, A and B. To find a point equally far from both, open the compass to one chosen distance.",
    "L4": "Draw an arc from A, then keep the same opening and draw an arc from B. Their crossing point is equally far from A and B.",
    "L5": "Use arcs above and below AB, and you get two such points. Changing the same opening gives more points.",
    "L6": "All these points lie on the line through the midpoint of AB at right angles to AB. The midpoint is not the only one.",
    "L7": "Check your understanding: a rectangle has diagonal AC equal to 10 centimetres. How long is diagonal BD?"
   },
